    <string name="title_safety">SAFETY TIPS</string>
    <string name="safety_ventilation">VENTILATION</string>
    <string name="safety_ventilation_body">Work with a cracked window or an extractor running. Developer and fixer release vapours that build up quickly in a small closed bathroom.</string>
    <string name="safety_gloves">GLOVES AND EYE PROTECTION</string>
    <string name="safety_gloves_body">Nitrile gloves and glasses cost little and stop the two injuries that actually happen: a splash in the eye and repeated skin contact with developer.</string>
    <string name="safety_stop">STOP BATH FUMES</string>
    <string name="safety_stop_body">Acetic stop bath smells sharp for a reason. Pour it with your face away from the tank and keep the bottle capped between rolls.</string>
    <string name="safety_fixer">FIXER DISPOSAL</string>
    <string name="safety_fixer_body">Used fixer carries dissolved silver and does not belong in a drain. Collect it in a labelled bottle and hand it to a chemical waste point.</string>
    <string name="safety_silver">SILVER RECOVERY</string>
    <string name="safety_silver_body">Steel wool in a sealed container drops the silver out of spent fixer over a few days, which makes the waste much easier to hand over.</string>
    <string name="safety_storage">STORAGE AND LABELLING</string>
    <string name="safety_storage_body">Label every bottle with the chemical, the dilution and the mix date. An unlabelled brown bottle is the most common cause of a ruined roll.</string>
    <string name="safety_spill">SPILL RESPONSE</string>
    <string name="safety_spill_body">Keep paper towels and a jug of clean water within reach. Flush skin for several minutes and rinse the floor before the solution dries to dust.</string>
    <string name="safety_food">KEEP CHEMISTRY AWAY FROM FOOD</string>
    <string name="safety_food_body">Never use kitchen measuring jugs for chemistry and never store bottles beside food. Dedicate the graduates to the darkroom and mark them clearly.</string>

